{% comment %}
  The body of the Size Guide dialog.

  The chart used to be one page per template - templates/product.*.json each
  pointed the variant picker's `size_guide_page` setting at "size-guide" or
  "kids-size-guide". That is the right default and it stays, but it cannot be
  the only answer: a kaftan, a pair of trousers and a cushion cover on the same
  template need different charts, and adding a template per chart means adding
  a template per chart forever.

  So the chart is resolved per product from metafields, and the template page
  becomes the fallback underneath them. Four keys, in the `custom` namespace:

    custom.size_chart_title   single line text   dialog heading
    custom.size_chart_image   file (image)       a chart supplied as artwork
    custom.size_chart         rich text          a chart written as copy
    custom.size_chart_page    page reference     a chart written once as a page
    custom.size_chart_note    multi-line text    the line under the chart

  How they combine:

  - Anything product-level set at all means the template page is not used. That
    keeps "this product has its own chart" a single decision rather than a
    merge the merchant has to reason about.
  - Within product-level, all three render, in the order image, copy, page.
    A brand whose designer sends a JPG and whose copywriter sends two lines of
    "measure across the chest" gets both, in that order, without a code change.
  - The note always renders last, template page or not. It is the one line that
    is genuinely per product - "this style runs small" - while the chart it
    sits under is usually shared.

  Reuse across a catalogue is what `size_chart_page` is for: point fifty
  products at one "Womens tops size chart" page and edit it in one place. The
  image and copy keys are for the product that is genuinely its own case.

  A product with nothing set and a template with no page renders no dialog and
  no link - sections/main-product.liquid and snippets/product-variant-picker
  .liquid both gate on the same three keys, so the link never opens onto an
  empty box.

  Accepts:
  - product: {Object} product object.
  - block:   {Object} the variant_picker block, carrying the template fallback
              page and the label used when nothing supplies a title.

  Usage:
  {% render 'pdp-size-chart', product: product, block: block %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign mf_image = product.metafields.custom.size_chart_image
  assign mf_copy = product.metafields.custom.size_chart
  assign mf_page = product.metafields.custom.size_chart_page
  assign mf_note = product.metafields.custom.size_chart_note
  assign mf_title = product.metafields.custom.size_chart_title.value | default: product.metafields.custom.size_chart_title

  assign template_page = block.settings.size_guide_page

  comment
    One "does this product bring its own chart" answer, computed once and used
    for both the branch below and the heading. Three conditions written out
    three times is how the link and the dialog drift apart.
  endcomment
  assign has_own = false
  if mf_image != blank or mf_copy != blank or mf_page != blank
    assign has_own = true
  endif

  comment
    Heading, most specific first: an explicit title, then the title of whichever
    page supplies the chart, then the link's own label. The page title is worth
    preferring over the label because "Womens tops size chart" says more than
    "Size Guide" once a store has several.
  endcomment
  assign heading = mf_title
  if heading == blank
    if has_own
      assign heading = mf_page.value.title
    else
      assign heading = template_page.title
    endif
  endif
  if heading == blank
    assign heading = block.settings.size_guide_label | default: 'Size Guide'
  endif
-%}

<h2 class="h2">{{ heading | escape }}</h2>

<div class="pdp-sizechart">
  {%- if has_own -%}
    {%- if mf_image != blank -%}
      <div class="pdp-sizechart__image">
        {%- render 'pdp-metafield', field: mf_image -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if mf_copy != blank -%}
      <div class="pdp-sizechart__copy">
        {%- render 'pdp-metafield', field: mf_copy -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      A page's content is output rather than handed to pdp-metafield, because
      the page is reached through a reference here and that snippet's
      page_reference branch expects the metafield, not the page. Same HTML
      either way.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if mf_page != blank -%}
      <div class="pdp-sizechart__copy">{{ mf_page.value.content }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- else -%}
    <div class="pdp-sizechart__copy">{{ template_page.content }}</div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if mf_note != blank -%}
    <div class="pdp-sizechart__note">
      {%- render 'pdp-metafield', field: mf_note -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
